---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { frames, frameThemes, pages } from '../data/pages';
import { imageFor, pageHref, frameHref, stats } from '../lib/site';

const s = stats();
const overview = imageFor(0, 0);
const collage = [imageFor(1, 3), imageFor(4, 7), imageFor(1, 6)];
const signed = pages.filter((p) => p.kinds.includes('Signed artist’s proof'));
---
<Base section="home">
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap hero-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">An exhibition collection · 1961 – 1969</p>
        <h1>Famous French Arts</h1>
        <p class="sub">French art stamps, proofs and covers, assembled and hand-illustrated by <strong>Milton Kohn</strong> and exhibited internationally by Milton &amp; Janet Kohn.</p>
        <p class="lede">
          Each album page follows one stamp from the engraver’s signed proof to the deluxe proof, the first day of issue and mail
          that actually went through the post. Kohn surrounded the stamps with his own lettering, hand-drawn postmarks and colour-pencil
          sketches of the places they depict.
        </p>
        <div class="btn-row">
          <a class="btn" href={frameHref(1)}>Begin with Frame 1 →</a>
          <a class="btn ghost" href="#about">About the collection</a>
        </div>
        <div class="stats">
          <div class="stat"><b>{s.pages}</b><span>album pages shown</span></div>
          <div class="stat"><b>{s.signed}</b><span>signed artist’s proofs</span></div>
          <div class="stat"><b>{s.deluxe}</b><span>deluxe proofs</span></div>
          <div class="stat"><b>{s.issues}</b><span>stamp issues, 1961–69</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-collage" aria-hidden="true">
        {collage.map((img) => (
          <figure><img src={img.thumb} alt="" width="640" height="780" loading="eager" /></figure>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="about" style="background: var(--paper-2);">
    <div class="wrap split narrow-left">
      <a href={overview.lg} class="mounted" style="display:block">
        <img src={overview.md} alt="The collection’s title page, hand-lettered “Famous French Arts”, with Milton Kohn’s introduction" width={overview.width} height={overview.height} loading="lazy" />
      </a>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">About the collection</p>
        <h2>“An education in philately, and one in art as well”</h2>
        <p>
          The exhibit opens with Kohn’s own statement of purpose, lettered by hand on the title page. He describes a <em>topical</em>
          collection: works by the world’s great artists as they appear on French stamps, told through material
          most collectors never see.
        </p>
        <blockquote class="hand">
          <p>This collection is topical in nature and it contains some of the works of the world’s renowned artists which is now shown on French art stamps. This collection shows the seldom seen artists proofs (of which there are only a few). It has essays, trial color proofs, imperfs, errors, first day covers, commercial covers, hand drawn cancels and hand drawn sketches that pertain to the subject or philately.</p>
          <p>For the average person it is not only an education in philately, but one in art as well. There are many facets of knowledge that philately can lead you to; this is just one. This French art collection has been a most relaxing subject I have ever enjoyed.</p>
          <cite>Milton Kohn, title page</cite>
        </blockquote>
        <p>
          The stamps belong to the French post office’s celebrated fine-art series. Launched in 1961 under André Malraux, then
          Minister of Cultural Affairs, it came to be called the <em>Musée Imaginaire</em>, an “imaginary museum” of
          masterpieces in miniature, each engraved by hand and printed in up to six colours on a rotary recess press.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="frames">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">The exhibit</p>
      <h2>Eight exhibition frames</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        The pages are grouped as they hung in their original exhibition frames. Frames 1–4 are shown here; frames 5–8 will be added as they are photographed.
      </p>
      <div class="frames" style="margin-top:28px">
        {frames.map((f) =>
          f.pages.length ? (
            <a class="frame-card" href={frameHref(f.number)}>
              <div class="thumbs">
                {f.pages.slice(1, 4).map((p) => (
                  <img src={imageFor(p.frame, p.index).thumb} alt="" width="640" height="780" loading="lazy" />
                ))}
              </div>
              <div class="body">
                <span class="num">Frame {f.number} · {f.pages.length} pages</span>
                <h3>{frameThemes[f.number].title}</h3>
                <p>{frameThemes[f.number].detail}</p>
              </div>
            </a>
          ) : (
            <div class="frame-card pending">
              <div class="thumbs">{f.number}</div>
              <div class="body">
                <span class="num">Frame {f.number}</span>
                <p>Being photographed. Coming soon.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          )
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="background: var(--paper-2);">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">Highlights</p>
      <h2>Proofs signed by the engravers</h2>
      <p class="lede">
        Artist’s proofs were pulled in tiny numbers from the finished die and signed in pencil by the engraver. The collection
        holds {signed.length}, signed by Pierre Gandon, Claude Durrens, René Cottet and Jacques Combet. Each signature
        matches the engraver named in the catalogue record for that stamp.
      </p>
      <div class="page-grid" style="margin-top:28px">
        {signed.map((p) => (
          <a class="page-card" href={pageHref(p)}>
            <div class="img"><img src={imageFor(p.frame, p.index).thumb} alt="" width="640" height="780" loading="lazy" /></div>
            <div class="meta">
              <span class="n">Frame {p.frame} · Page {p.index}</span>
              <h3>{p.title}</h3>
            </div>
          </a>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="collectors">
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="eyebrow">The collectors</p>
        <h2>Milton &amp; Janet Kohn</h2>
        <p>
          <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Milton_Kohn">Milton Mendel Kohn</a> (1912–2001) was a Chicago architect,
          trained at the Armour Institute of Technology (now the Illinois Institute of Technology). He took up stamp collecting
          in the mid-1960s, on his doctor’s advice to find a relaxing hobby, and the closing line of his title page says the
          same. He is best known as a collector of Holocaust memorabilia, a collection now at the Florida Holocaust
          Museum, where a gallery bears his wife Janet’s name.
        </p>
        <p>
          <em>Famous French Arts</em> was a separate project. Milton and Janet Kohn exhibited it internationally in the 1960s and
          1970s. Every page shows his architect’s training: the ruled double borders, the steady capital lettering and the carefully
          mounted proofs. <strong>All of the artwork on the pages was drawn by Milton Kohn himself</strong>, including the
          calligraphic headings, the hand-drawn copies of first-day postmarks, the magnified drawings that explain colour
          varieties, and the colour-pencil sketches of French streets, cathedrals and craftsmen.
        </p>
        <a class="btn ghost" href="/guide/">How to read an album page →</a>
      </div>
      <figure class="mounted" style="margin:0">
        <img src={imageFor(2, 6).md} alt="Album page with the Notre-Dame stamp set inside a Gothic window drawn by Milton Kohn" width="1600" height="1925" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;color:var(--ink-soft);padding:10px 4px 2px">
          Frame 2, page 6: Kohn mounted the Notre-Dame stamp as a pane of his own hand-drawn Gothic window.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
